/*! \page page_inputs Input variables

    \tableofcontents
    \latexonly
        \textit{\scriptsize Footnotes are situated at the end of the chapter}
    \endlatexonly

    This page describes the input variables of PB3D and POST.

    Most inputs are read from the user input file, which holds the Fortran namelist \c inputdata_PB3D for PB3D or \c inputdata_POST for POST.
    Every variable that is not set in the file keeps its default value.
    A name in the file that is not part of the namelist stops the program with the error "Cannot read user-provided file".
    The other inputs are options on the command line.
    PB3D and POST are called as
    \verbatim
    PB3D USER_INPUT EQUILIBRIUM_INPUT [OPTIONS]
    POST USER_INPUT PB3D_OUTPUT [OPTIONS]
    \endverbatim
    where the options take the form <tt>--[option_name]</tt>, sometimes followed by values.
    If the user input file cannot be opened, the program uses the defaults for all variables.
    \see See init_files() and open_input().

    For both PB3D and POST, both types of input are discussed.

    Some general remarks:
        - There is some fault detection built into the routines. These distinguish between minor faults, which trigger a warning but do not stop execution, and major faults, which trigger an error that stops execution.
            \see See writo().
        - The data type \c real refers to double precision real, defined by num_vars.dp. For integers, standard precision is used. A type such as \c int(2) is an array of two values, given in the input file as two numbers separated by a space.
        - Angles are given in units of \f$\pi\f$, marked \f$\left[\pi\right]\f$.
        - PB3D takes a file with the extension <tt>.nc</tt> as a VMEC file, which gives <tt>eq_style = 1</tt>, and a file that starts with an integer as a HELENA file, which gives <tt>eq_style = 2</tt>. Several defaults depend on \c eq_style. POST reads \c eq_style from the PB3D output.
        - PB3D limits the tolerances \c tol_rich, \c tol_zero and \c tol_SLEPC to the range from \c min_tol \f$= 10^{-12}\f$ to \c max_tol \f$= 10^{-3}\f$, which are hard-coded in read_input_opts().
        - If <tt>eq_style = 2</tt> (i.e. HELENA), the total maximum memory available must be enough to perform the first Richardson level in a single equilibrium job (see \ref page_overview).
        - PB3D computes the vacuum response only for a free boundary, <tt>BC_style(2) = 2</tt>. No input chooses the vacuum style. PB3D takes the field-line 3-D style for a VMEC equilibrium and the axisymmetric style for a HELENA equilibrium (see \ref fni37 "37").

    \section inputs_PB3D PB3D Inputs

