    \subsection inputs_POST_cmd Command-line inputs

    <table>
    <caption id="inputs_POST_cmd_tab">Table 4. POST command-line options</caption>
    <tr>
        <th> input parameter </th><th> explanation </th><th> argument </th><th> note </th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> \c test </td><td> test mode \ldebug </td><td></td><td> (\ref fni28 "28") </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> \c no_plots </td><td> do not produce any output plots </td><td></td><td></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> \c no_output </td><td> do not write the messages of writo() </td><td></td><td> (\ref fni29 "29") </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> \c do_execute_command_line </td><td> run the external command line shell commands during execution </td><td></td><td> (\ref fni30 "30") </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> \c mem_info </td><td> produce memory profiles for each process \ldebug </td><td></td><td> (\ref fni31 "31") </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> \c swap_angles </td><td> swap \f$\theta\f$ and \f$\zeta\f$ of the slab plots (\c plot_grid_style \f$1\f$ or \f$2\f$) for <tt>POST_style = 2</tt> </td><td></td><td></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> \c compare_tor_pos </td><td> compare \f$\vec{B}\f$, \f$\vec{J}\f$ and \f$\vec{\kappa}\f$ from \c plot_B, \c plot_J and \c plot_kappa at different toroidal positions, as well as the difference in position </td><td> \f$R\f$ and \f$Z\f$ of the origin of the geometrical poloidal angle (<tt>RZ_0(2)</tt>) </td><td> (\ref fni32 "32") </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    \note
        -# \anchor fni1 The normalized flux is either the poloidal (<tt>use_pol_flux_F = .true.</tt>) or toroidal (<tt>use_pol_flux_F = .false.</tt>), divided by its value at the boundary. PB3D raises a negative \c min_r_sol to 0 and lowers a \c max_r_sol above 1 to 1, and it stops if \c min_r_sol is not below \c max_r_sol. For VMEC, PB3D warns when \c min_r_sol reaches the magnetic axis.
        -# \anchor fni2 check_X_modes() uses \c tol_norm for \c X_style 1, when it checks whether there exists a normal range in which each of the modes resonates. PB3D limits \c tol_norm to the range from 0 to 1.
        -# \anchor fni3 This is the number of parallel points at the first Richardson level. At every next level, the number of intervals is doubled, so that \f$n\f$ points become \f$2n-1\f$. PB3D raises \c min_n_par_X to 20 if it is smaller. For <tt>magn_int_style = 2</tt> (Simpson 3/8 rule), PB3D raises it to the next number of the form \f$4 + 3k\f$.
        -# \anchor fni4 The absolute value of \c prim_X must be at least \min_nm_X (\c min_nm_X in X_vars), because PB3D uses an expansion for high mode numbers.
        -# \anchor fni5 \c X_style is not an input. PB3D derives it from the other inputs. If \c min_sec_X and \c max_sec_X are set, the user prescribes the range of secondary mode numbers, and PB3D sets <tt>X_style = 1</tt> (prescribed). If they are not set, PB3D sets <tt>X_style = 2</tt> (fast), and at every normal position it uses the \c n_mod_X secondary modes that are closest to resonance. Setting \c min_sec_X and \c max_sec_X together with \c n_mod_X stops PB3D with an error. With <tt>X_style = 2</tt>, a mode that leaves the range of modes at one normal position and a mode that enters it are not coupled, so the solution should be checked by doubling \c n_mod_X once.
        -# \anchor fni6 PB3D computes the normalization constants, with the exception of \c rho_0, in the routine calc_normalization_const(). A value given in the input file replaces the computed value. For HELENA equilibria, PB3D always uses normalization, and it sets \c use_normalization to <tt>.true.</tt> with a warning.
        -# \anchor fni7 In store_results(), PB3D marks an eigenvalue \f$\lambda\f$ as unphysical if the ratio \f$\left|\mathrm{Im}\,\lambda / \mathrm{Re}\,\lambda\right|\f$ exceeds 10 times \c tol_SLEPC, if \f$\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda\f$ lies within a relative distance \f$10^{-3}\f$ of \c EV_BC, or if \f$\left|\lambda\right| > 10^{40}\f$ (this eigenvalue and all following ones). Without \c retain_all_sol, PB3D leaves these eigenvalues out. With \c retain_all_sol, it keeps them and comments them out in the eigenvalue output file, with the reason.
        -# \anchor fni8 This needs to be between 1 and the maximum level that was obtained in a previous solution, plus 1, and it should not exceed \c max_it_rich. For technical HDF5 reasons, if Richardson restart is used, the same input file parameters should be used for \c max_tot_mem and the same number of processes should be used. PB3D stops if the output it continues from was written by a version older than \c min_PB3D_version (num_vars).
        -# \anchor fni9 For the case of num_ops.calc_zero_hh(), at every iteration it is checked whether the correction approximation is better than the original one. If it is not, the correction is relaxed by a factor half. This is done \c max_nr_backtracks_HH times.
        -# \anchor fni10 See set_BC(). The inner end accepts only <tt>BC_style(1) = 1</tt>, and the edge accepts <tt>BC_style(2) = 1</tt> (fixed boundary) or <tt>BC_style(2) = 2</tt> (free boundary). Any other value stops PB3D with an error message. For the points where the perturbation is set to zero, PB3D sets the diagonal of \f$\overline{\text{A}}\f$ to \c EV_BC, the diagonal of \f$\overline{\text{B}}\f$ to 1 and the other elements of their rows to zero, so that these rows give eigenvalues equal to \c EV_BC.
        -# \anchor fni11 See calc_normalization_const(). The choice applies to VMEC equilibria only; HELENA equilibria always use the MISHKA normalization.
        -# \anchor fni12 See solve_ev_system_slepc()
        -# \anchor fni13 See resonance_plot().
        -# \anchor fni14 See magn_grid_plot().
        -# \anchor fni15 See b_plot(). The output is done in various coordinate system, as explained in calc_vec_comp().
        -# \anchor fni16 See j_plot(). The output is done in various coordinate system, as explained in calc_vec_comp().
        -# \anchor fni17 See kappa_plot(). The output is done in various coordinate system, as explained in calc_vec_comp().
        -# \anchor fni18 See flux_q_plot().
        -# \anchor fni19 For <tt>eq_style = 1</tt> (VMEC), the equilibrium can be 3-D, so by default <tt>n_theta_plot = 201</tt> and <tt>n_zeta_plot = 51</tt>. For <tt>eq_style = 2</tt> (HELENA), the equilibrium is axisymmetric, so by default <tt>n_theta_plot = 501</tt> and <tt>n_zeta_plot = 1</tt>. If \c n_theta_plot is 1, \c max_theta_plot is set to \c min_theta_plot. If \c n_zeta_plot is 1, \c min_zeta_plot and \c max_zeta_plot are both set to their average.
        -# \anchor fni20 For <tt>eq_style = 1</tt> (VMEC), the equilibrium can be 3-D, so by default <tt>min_zeta_plot = 0</tt> and <tt>max_zeta_plot = 2</tt>. For <tt>eq_style = 2</tt> (HELENA), the equilibrium is axisymmetric, so by default <tt>min_zeta_plot = max_zeta_plot = 0</tt>.
        -# \anchor fni21 This refers to the external plots, which are complementary to the HDF5 plots. See draw_ex(). Another value than 1 or 2 is replaced by 1, with a warning.
        -# \anchor fni22 By default, POST treats the highest Richardson level found in the PB3D output (see find_max_rich_lvl()). A requested level above it stops POST with an error, and a level below it gives a warning. If the output holds no solution, POST only produces the output that does not depend on the solution, and it switches off \c plot_sol_xi, \c plot_sol_Q and \c plot_E_rec. \c PB3D_rich_lvl should be positive. When the output holds a solution, POST takes a value that is not positive as the requested level, like any other value. POST stops if the PB3D output was written by a version older than \c min_PB3D_version (num_vars).
        -# \anchor fni23 The \c POST_style determines whether the post-processing is done on an extended, new grid, or on the PB3D internal grid. On top of this, through \c plot_grid_style the user can set the output grid for the post-processing. Note that some combinations of styles do not make sense.
        -# \anchor fni24 The plot range in the normal direction is the part of the solution range of PB3D, from \c min_r_sol to \c max_r_sol, that lies between \c min_r_plot and \c max_r_plot. With the defaults 0 and 1, it is the whole solution range. Restricting it is useful, for example, to reduce computation time.
        -# \anchor fni25 For each solution independently and sequentially, POST divides \c pert_mult_factor_POST by the maximum amplitude of the normal displacement of the solution, multiplies the Cartesian components of the displacement by the result, and adds them to the equilibrium Cartesian grid to show the perturbation in 3-D. POST ignores a factor whose absolute value is below \c tol_zero. The factor acts when \c plot_sol_xi or \c plot_sol_Q is <tt>.true.</tt>, and POST then also plots \f$\vec{\xi}\f$.
        -# \anchor fni26 These quantities are calculated in sol_utilities.calc_xuq(). This is done for a time sequence, currently hard-coded in plot_sol_vec(), depending on whether the solution is stable or unstable.
        -# \anchor fni27 POST performs the energy reconstruction in calc_e(), through decompose_energy(), when \c plot_E_rec is <tt>.true.</tt>.
        -# \anchor fni28 Equivalent use is through <tt>-t</tt>. This option works only in a debug build (see \ref page_installation); otherwise the program prints a warning and ignores it. If this is used, \c do_execute_command_line should be set to true as well. See generic_tests().
        -# \anchor fni29 This is used internally as well, to suppress certain output. See run_driver_x() and run_driver_post().
        -# \anchor fni30 This can fail on local systems, and generally works badly on computational clusters, where the computational nodes often don't have the external tools (see \c ex_plot_style) installed. By default, an output file is created for the shell commands, which can be executed afterwards. See \ref output_file_shell.
        -# \anchor fni31 This option works only in a debug build. It calls get_mem_usage() and stores the result at every allocation and deallocation of an \c eq_1_type, \c eq_2_type, \c X_1_type, \c X_2_type, \c vac_type or \c sol_type, as well as for input quantities. Furthermore at every user output with writo(), this is also done, and the output is prepended to the output string. Also, the output is written to a file. See \ref output_file_mem.
        -# \anchor fni32 This is useful to calculate the ripple in these quantities. The option takes two numbers, the \f$R\f$ and \f$Z\f$ value of the origin of the geometrical poloidal angle that is used to calculate distances, for example <tt>--compare_tor_pos 3.0 0.0</tt>. POST stops with an error unless <tt>n_zeta_plot = 3</tt>, <tt>POST_style = 1</tt>, \c min_r_plot is positive, <tt>max_r_plot = 1</tt>, <tt>min_theta_plot = 0</tt> and <tt>max_theta_plot = 2</tt>. The poloidal projection of the middle toroidal point should lie in the middle between the poloidal projections of the two others. See grid_utilities.calc_tor_diff().
        -# \anchor fni33 Jumping straight to the solution is useful if a simulation already calculated all the necessary quantities to set up the solution matrices \f$\overline{\text{A}}\f$ and \f$\overline{\text{B}}\f$ in \f$\overline{\text{A}} \vec{X} = \lambda \overline{\text{B}} \vec{X}\f$ and the solution needs to be redone, for example because a calculation was attempted with a method that did not converge.
        -# \anchor fni34 This is a rather complicated procedure with many possibilities that is intended for expert use. It works only for HELENA equilibria and with one process. See create_vmec_input().
        -# \anchor fni35 Using the toroidal flux is \b experimental and \b untested. For HELENA equilibria, PB3D always uses the poloidal flux, and it sets \c use_pol_flux_F to <tt>.true.</tt> with a warning.
        -# \anchor fni36 Have a look in calc_norm_range(). \c X_grid_style \f$1\f$ is generally too coarse, while \c X_grid_style \f$2\f$ is too fine. \c X_grid_style \f$3\f$ is a compromise between the two. It only makes sense for \c X_style 2 (fast), and PB3D stops with an error if it is combined with \c X_style 1. In the enrichment, PB3D adds normal points to the equilibrium grid until the quantity \f$n q\f$ (when using poloidal flux) or \f$m \iota\f$ (when using toroidal flux) changes by at most \c max_njq_change between two neighbouring points.
        -# \anchor fni37 PB3D computes the vacuum response, in calc_vac_res(), only for a free boundary, <tt>BC_style(2) = 2</tt>; with <tt>BC_style(2) = 1</tt>, it skips the vacuum. The vacuum style follows from the equilibrium. For a VMEC equilibrium, PB3D uses the field-line 3-D style (style 1): a boundary-element method on the points of the field lines on the plasma boundary. It needs several field lines, so a free-boundary VMEC run with <tt>alpha_style = 1</tt> stops with an error; PB3D computes the vacuum at every Richardson level. For a HELENA equilibrium, PB3D uses the axisymmetric style (style 2), with toroidal functions on the boundary of the poloidal cross-section, and it computes the vacuum once, at the first Richardson level of the run (\c rich_restart_lvl). PB3D solves the boundary-element system with STRUMPACK if it was built with STRUMPACK, and with the ScaLAPACK LU factorization otherwise (see \ref page_installation).
        -# \anchor fni38 The check runs inside calc_vac_res(), so only when PB3D computes the vacuum (see \ref fni37 "37"). check_vac_exact() tests the boundary-element matrices on the plasma boundary of the equilibrium with potentials that are harmonic inside the boundary (the constant, the Cartesian coordinates \f$x\f$, \f$y\f$, \f$z\f$, and \f$x^2 - y^2\f$) and with potentials that are harmonic outside it and decay (a point source on the magnetic axis and a ring of point sources along the axis). It prints the residuals of the Green identities, the errors of the exterior solution and the differences between the assembled diagonals and the alternative ones on lines of the log that start with <tt>CHECKVAC</tt>. It also writes the values at the boundary points to files <tt>check_vac_*.dat</tt> in the run directory.
        -# \anchor fni39 For the field-line style, the indirect diagonals follow from the row sums for the constant and the linear interior harmonics; for the axisymmetric style, they follow from a least-squares fit to known potentials (diag_indirect()). The direct diagonals use the corner term and the integral of the kernel over the cell of the node (calc_diag_direct() for the field-line style). Style 1 is the more accurate of the two, and PB3D prints a warning that style 2 is a diagnostic, not meant for production runs, when it is used with a free boundary. With \c check_vac, PB3D reports the difference between the assembled diagonal and the other one at every point. A value other than 1 or 2 stops PB3D with an error message.
        -# \anchor fni40 POST plots the vacuum potential only for a free boundary; for a fixed boundary it switches \c plot_vac_pot off. It evaluates the potential in the planes of constant \f$\zeta\f$ of the plasma plots (\c n_zeta_plot planes from \c min_zeta_plot to \c max_zeta_plot), on a grid of \c n_vac_plot(1) by \c n_vac_plot(2) points in \f$R\f$ and \f$Z\f$. If \c max_Rvac_plot is not larger than \c min_Rvac_plot, which is the case for the defaults, the range in \f$R\f$ is the range of the boundary points widened on both sides by half the larger of the widths of the boundary in \f$R\f$ and in \f$Z\f$, and it starts at no less than 0.1 times the smallest \f$R\f$ of the boundary; the same rule with \c min_Zvac_plot and \c max_Zvac_plot sets the range in \f$Z\f$. A range given by the user must have a positive \c min_Rvac_plot. The limits are in metres. For a HELENA equilibrium without a user-given \c R_0, PB3D sets <tt>R_0 = 1</tt>, and the limits are then in units of the length scale of the HELENA equilibrium. See vac_pot_plot() for the output.
        -# \anchor fni41 The four values are the number of the first unstable solutions, of the last unstable solutions, of the first stable solutions and of the last stable solutions that POST plots. A negative value means all solutions of that range. See find_stab_ranges().
        -# \anchor fni42 PB3D recognizes these PETSc and SLEPC options so that it does not warn about them. PETSc and SLEPC read them from the command line themselves; SLEPC reads the solver options in EPSSetFromOptions(), which get_solution() calls after the solver has been set up, so that they override the settings of \c solver_SLEPC_style, \c tol_SLEPC and \c max_it_SLEPC. These options start with a single dash. The factorization package is called <tt>-st_pc_factor_mat_solver_type</tt> in current PETSc versions. PB3D does not recognize that name, so it prints a warning for the option and for its value, but SLEPC still reads it.
        -# \anchor fni43 For <tt>alpha_style = 1</tt>, PB3D follows the single field line with label \c alpha from \c min_par_X to \c max_par_X, and it sets \c n_alpha to 1. For <tt>alpha_style = 2</tt>, PB3D uses \c n_alpha field lines with the labels \f$\alpha_j = 2\pi (j-1)/n_\alpha\f$, \f$j = 1 \ldots n_\alpha\f$, ignores \c alpha, and resets \c min_par_X and \c max_par_X to 0 and 2 if they do not span one poloidal turn. A free boundary with a VMEC equilibrium needs <tt>alpha_style = 2</tt>. PB3D warns when \c n_alpha and the number of field periods \f$N_\text{fp}\f$ of the VMEC equilibrium have a common factor, because the sum over the field lines then adds the toroidal harmonic of order \f$\mathrm{LCM}(n_\alpha, N_\text{fp})\f$ of the equilibrium to the average; \c n_alpha should be chosen coprime to \f$N_\text{fp}\f$. For the field-line 3-D vacuum, the convergence studies in README.md of the code recommend at least 128 field lines and about 36 parallel points per poloidal wavelength of the perturbation. For HELENA equilibria, which are axisymmetric, PB3D warns if \c n_alpha is larger than 1 or if \c min_par_X and \c max_par_X do not span exactly one poloidal turn.
        -# \anchor fni44 PB3D raises \c n_r_sol to at least <tt>6 norm_disc_prec_sol + 2</tt>, and to at least <tt>(2 norm_disc_prec_sol + 1)</tt> times the number of MPI processes. For a free boundary with <tt>norm_disc_prec_sol</tt> 2 or 3, the last points of the solution grid carry the weights of num_utilities.calc_end_weights() in the normal integral.
        -# \anchor fni45 A value below 1 is replaced by the number of MPI processes, and a value above the number of MPI processes is lowered to it, with a warning.
        -# \anchor fni46 Up to 100 values can be given. If fewer values than \c max_it_rich are given, the remaining levels get \f$10^{-5}\f$; values beyond \c max_it_rich are discarded.
*/
